---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: author.name,
    section: "Brand Reviews",
    tags: [
      "arbonne protein powder",
      "arbonne protein shake",
      "arbonne protein powder ingredients",
      "arbonne vanilla protein powder"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
  stickyCta={{
    text: "See Our Top Plant-Based Protein Picks",
    link: "/best-plant-based-protein-powder/",
  }}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <ArticleHeader kicker={"Brand Review"} badge={"Label-Checked"} author={author} dates={dates} mobileAd={false}>
      <Fragment slot="title">Arbonne Protein Powder Review</Fragment>
      What is in Arbonne's FeelFit and Simply1 shakes, whether they are third-party tested, what they really cost per gram of protein, and which Amazon powders do the same job for less.
    </ArticleHeader>

    <TableOfContents>
      <a href="#short-answer">Short Answer</a>
      <a href="#top-picks">The Products</a>
      <a href="#names">Product Names</a>
      <a href="#ingredients">Ingredients</a>
      <a href="#testing">Third-Party Testing</a>
      <a href="#cost">Cost per 25 g</a>
      <a href="#amazon">On Amazon?</a>
      <a href="#taste">Taste and Mixing</a>
      <a href="#vs">Arbonne vs Rivals</a>
      <a href="#who">Who Should Buy</a>
      <a href="#mistakes">Common Mistakes</a>
      <a href="#faq">FAQ</a>
    </TableOfContents>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="lede">
          Arbonne protein powder is a clean-label vegan shake with 20 g of pea, cranberry and rice protein per serving, but it costs about $4.21 per 25 g of protein on Amazon and Arbonne names no sport-grade third-party testing for it. The answer changes if you value the probiotics and vitamin blend. Below: the label, the testing question, the real cost and three cheaper swaps.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Arbonne Protein Powder: The Short Answer
          </h2>

          <p>
            Arbonne protein powder is a decent vegan shake at a premium price. Both current versions, FeelFit and the lower-sugar FeelFit Simply1, give 20 g of protein from peas, cranberries and rice per serving, per Arbonne's labels and listings. At the $101 Arbonne charges for Simply1 in its own Amazon store, that is about $3.37 a shake and about $4.21 per 25 g of protein. Arbonne does not name NSF Certified for Sport, Informed Sport or any other independent contaminant testing for the shakes.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Buy it if</strong> you already like Arbonne, want a vegan shake with added vitamins (and, in the original FeelFit, named probiotic strains), and the cost per gram does not matter to you. <strong>Skip it if</strong> you want more protein per scoop, a sport certification, or a lower bill: the Amazon alternatives below give 21 to 30 g of protein for $1.35 to $1.90 per 25 g. Our wider shortlist is in the <a href="/best-plant-based-protein-powder/">best plant-based protein powders</a> guide.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Question</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Answer (per label at the time of writing)</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Protein per serving</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">20 g (FeelFit and Simply1)</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Protein sources</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Pea, cranberry and rice protein</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Sweetener</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Simply1: stevia only. Earlier FeelFit labels: cane sugar plus stevia</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Third-party testing</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">No NSF, Informed Sport or Informed Choice named by Arbonne</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Price on Amazon</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$101 for 30 servings (Simply1), sold by Arbonne</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Cost per 25 g of protein</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $4.21 (Simply1, Amazon price seen)</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Arbonne Shakes and the Closest Amazon Alternatives
          </h2>

          <p>
            Two Arbonne shakes as sold in Arbonne's own Amazon store, then three vegan powders that match the label for far less per gram. Prices are the Amazon prices we saw at the time of writing and change often.
          </p>

          {PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
            <ProductCard product={product} rank={index + 1} />
          ))}
        </div>

        <div id="names" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. What Is Arbonne Protein Powder Called Now?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Arbonne protein powder is currently sold as the FeelFit Pea Protein Shake, in an original version and a Simply1 version. If you are searching for Arbonne Essentials Protein Shake Mix, that is the older name for the same product family. Older Essentials labels listed an Arbonne protein matrix blend of pea, cranberry and rice protein, 20 g of protein in a two-scoop serving and cane sugar as a main ingredient. The protein blend has carried over into FeelFit; the extras and the sweetening have changed.
          </p>

          <p>
            The original FeelFit shake is the feature-heavy one. Arbonne's Amazon listing for the chocolate flavor states 20 g of protein, two probiotic strains (2 billion CFU of Bifidobacterium longum BB536 and 10 billion CFU of Lactobacillus rhamnosus GG) and 24 vitamins and minerals, including methylated forms of vitamin B12 and folate. Arbonne also says it is made without artificial colors, flavors or sweeteners and formulated without gluten, dairy, soy or high fructose corn syrup.
          </p>

          <p>
            FeelFit Simply1 is the stripped-back, lower-sugar version. Per its label at the time of writing, two scoops weigh about 31 g and give 20 g of protein for around 130 calories, with under 1 g of sugar and a few grams of carbohydrate. It is sweetened with stevia leaf extract only. Arbonne's Amazon store lists it in vanilla, chocolate and strawberry, and some consultants also list a coffee flavor.
          </p>

          <p>
            The third product people lump in with Arbonne protein powder is EssentialMeal, which is a meal replacement rather than a protein shake. Arbonne's listing states 25 g of vegan protein from pea, hemp seed, pumpkin seed, quinoa and rice, plus avocado oil for fat. At $112 for 30 servings on Amazon it costs more again. If a full meal shake is what you want, compare it with the options in our <a href="/best-meal-replacement-shake/">best meal replacement shake</a> guide and our <a href="/kachava-review/">Ka'Chava review</a> before you commit.
          </p>

          <p>
            Because the names and formulas have changed several times, the label on the bag you actually receive is the only reliable source. Regional versions differ too: Arbonne's Australian and UK shakes have used slightly different ingredient lists and nutrition figures from the US product. Everything on this page refers to the US labels and listings at the time of writing.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="ingredients" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. What Are the Arbonne Protein Powder Ingredients?
          </h2>

          <p>
            The core Arbonne protein powder ingredients are pea protein, cranberry protein and rice protein, followed by a sweetener, natural flavors, thickeners and a vitamin and mineral premix. Pea protein does most of the work. It is rich in lysine and leucine but low in methionine; rice protein fills some of that gap, which is why pea and rice blends are the standard formula across plant shakes. Cranberry protein, made from the seeds, is unusual and adds a small amount of protein plus some fiber and fat.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What is in the Arbonne vanilla protein powder?</h3>

          <p>
            The Arbonne vanilla protein powder you will most often find now is Simply1 Vanilla. Its sweetener is stevia, it has under 1 g of sugar per serving, and it carries Arbonne's vitamin and mineral blend. Older vanilla Essentials labels went further down the list: sunflower oil, natural vanilla flavor, inulin, maltodextrin, flax seed, stevia leaf extract and several gums, with 160 calories in a 45 g, two-scoop serving. If you have an older bag, that is the formula you are drinking.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How much sugar is in an Arbonne protein shake?</h3>

          <p>
            It depends entirely on the version. Simply1 has under 1 g of sugar per serving, per its label. The original FeelFit and the older Essentials mix used cane sugar plus stevia, and reviewers who list those labels report about 7 g of sugar and 160 calories a serving. That is not a lot for a whole day, but if you drink two shakes a day it adds 14 g of sugar, more than a third of the 25 to 36 g daily added-sugar limits the American Heart Association suggests. Read the panel on the current bag rather than assuming.
          </p>

          <p>
            Two more things on the label deserve attention. First, the vitamin and mineral premix: 23 to 24 nutrients at meaningful doses means Arbonne tells buyers not to exceed two shakes a day, and anyone already taking a multivitamin is doubling up. Second, the probiotics in the original FeelFit are named strains with stated counts, which is better than the vague probiotic blends many shakes use. If probiotics are the draw, our <a href="/best-protein-powder-with-probiotics/">protein powder with probiotics</a> guide has options with certifications attached.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE LABEL IN ONE LINE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">20 g of pea, cranberry and rice protein, plus vitamins</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Every current Arbonne protein shake gives 20 g of vegan protein. The differences are sugar (Simply1 has under 1 g), probiotics (original FeelFit only) and price.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="testing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Is Arbonne Protein Third-Party Tested?
          </h2>

          <p>
            No sport-grade third-party testing is named for Arbonne protein powder. We found no NSF Certified for Sport, Informed Sport or Informed Choice listing for FeelFit or Simply1, and Arbonne's Amazon listings do not claim one. Arbonne also does not publish batch certificates of analysis for heavy metals the way some plant brands do, and we found no independent lab results for its shakes.
          </p>

          <p>
            What Arbonne does have is worth stating accurately. The company is a Certified B Corporation; it was first certified in late 2019 and recertified in January 2023 with a score of 119.9, per Arbonne's own announcement. That is a company-wide assessment of environmental and social practices. It is not a test of what is in the powder, and it says nothing about lead, cadmium or banned substances. Product databases also list FeelFit as vegan and Non-GMO Project Verified, which are ingredient-sourcing certifications rather than contaminant tests.
          </p>

          <p>
            Why does this matter for a pea protein shake in particular? Plant proteins grown in soil tend to carry more heavy metals than dairy proteins, and Consumer Reports' 2025 protein testing found the highest lead levels in plant-based products. Arbonne was not among the products we saw named in that report, so there is no evidence either way. That absence is the point: without a certification or published lab data, you are relying on the brand's internal quality control.
          </p>

          <p>
            If testing is your main concern, the Garden of Life Sport card above is NSF Certified for Sport, per its listing, and our <a href="/best-protein-powder-without-heavy-metals/">protein powders without heavy metals</a> guide ranks plant options by published test data. For a broader look at what certifications do and do not prove, see <a href="/is-protein-powder-safe/">is protein powder safe</a>. Athletes subject to drug testing should treat any uncertified shake, Arbonne included, as unverified.
          </p>
        </div>

